<div class="r-site" data-r107-page data-family="about" data-modules="1,2,3,4,5"><figure class="r-about-campaign"><img src="{{ 'suiji-home-about-optimized.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="SUIJI design campaign concept" width="1200" height="1400" loading="eager" decoding="async" class="" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Studio campaign concept</figcaption></figure><header class="r-intro"><p class="r-eyebrow">About SUIJI</p><h1>Jewelry that changes with you.</h1><p>Since 2014, our work in handmade jewelry has been shaped by conversations with the people who wear it. SUIJI grew from a wish we heard often: to keep a design you love and find more ways to wear it.</p></header><section class="r-about-chapter"><h2>What SUIJI means</h2><div>
<p>The name SUIJI expresses the idea of following yourself. Your style can change from one day to the next while still feeling like you. We make room for that choice in the way our jewelry comes together.</p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="why-modular"><h2>Why structure</h2><div>
<p>In our custom work, some customers wanted a favorite charm to appear in matching earrings, a ring or a necklace. Others wanted to change a detail for a different outfit or occasion. Buying a whole new piece each time often meant buying the same parts again.</p>
<p>That experience led us to a modular approach: keep the base you love, choose another compatible charm, or carry a favorite detail across different clasp-equipped bases. The pieces can work together without making you start again with every new idea.</p>
<p>We want jewelry to be worn often and enjoyed over time. A piece chosen for a celebration can become part of an ordinary day; a favorite detail can find another place to be worn as your life changes. Our modular system is a way to keep those possibilities open.</p>

<p><strong>Designed to be worn.</strong> We design with the body in mind, considering proportions, contact, balance and movement as well as appearance.</p></div></section><section class="r-about-chapter"><h2>Core-Lock &amp; Modular System</h2><div>
<p>Bases give the jewelry its form and wearing position. Charms bring colour, texture and personal meaning. Core-Lock is the SUIJI multi-use clasp that connects compatible elements.</p>
<p>Every SUIJI charm fits bases equipped with the SUIJI multi-use clasp. Bare hoops and other components may need an additional attachment, so each listing explains what is supplied and what else is needed. A charm moves from one position to another; it does not become several charms at once.</p>
<p>During the later development of Core-Lock, French engineers specializing in springs contributed to refining the spring structure, mechanics and the pressure needed to operate the clasp.</p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="designer"><h2>A note from Chunyu</h2><figure><img src="{{ 'suiji-designer-chunyu-wen-v40.jpeg' | asset_url }}" alt="Chunyu Wen — SUIJI Brand &amp; Product" width="1200" height="1400" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class=""></figure><div>
<p class="r-designer-role">Chunyu Wen · Founder, Brand &amp; Product</p>
<p>I was born in a mining community, and even my name has a connection to the stones around me: Chunyu means “spring jade.” Searching for gemstones was one of the games we played as children. We would pick up beautiful stones much as other children might collect pebbles. I had no idea that some of them were valuable. I simply loved their colours and shapes. My fascination with jewelry began there, long before I imagined it could become my work.</p>
<p>I went on to study jewelry design at university. After graduating, my working life took me in several different directions: UI/UX design, branding, marketing and management, in France and the United States. I also studied brand management at INSEEC in France. These experiences taught me a great deal, but the dream I had carried since childhood kept returning. I often wondered whether I should come back to the work that felt closest to who I was.</p>
<p>A turning point in my life finally brought that question into focus. I decided to return to jewelry design and devote myself to what I truly loved. That was the beginning of my practice in bespoke fine jewelry.</p>
<p>Over the past decade, one of the happiest parts of this work has been seeing people enjoy the pieces I have designed for them. I am grateful for the conversations, the trust and the pleasure of making something that becomes part of someone’s life.</p>
<p>Those conversations also showed me something. A customer might love a particular design or charm and want to wear it as matching earrings, a ring or a necklace. Another might want a different detail for an evening or a change of outfit. Buying a whole new piece each time meant buying many of the same parts again, even when those parts were still loved and useful.</p>
<p>That is where SUIJI’s modular approach began: keep the base you enjoy wearing, change a compatible charm, and find another way to wear a detail you love. I want our jewelry to spend more time with you. A special occasion can be its beginning, with many ordinary days still to come.</p>
<p>France is part of my family life, too. My husband is French, we have a daughter, and I now share my time between France and China. When my daughter was little, she would already think carefully about the colours that connected her clothes and jewelry. I still see that attention in how she dresses for different occasions. It reminds me how early, and how naturally, we begin to make these choices our own.</p>
<p>My hope is that everyone will be able to have a hand in designing jewelry that feels unmistakably theirs. SUIJI gives a composition its structure; the ideas, details and meaning you bring are what make it personal.</p>
</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="studio"><h2>The studio</h2><div>
<p>Our studio brings people from different disciplines together to develop both design and technology. Designers, engineers, master artisans and experienced makers all have a part in refining how a SUIJI piece looks, connects and feels to wear.</p>
<p>We work with craftspeople who carry forward traditional handcraft and intangible cultural heritage techniques, while exploring contemporary methods such as advanced 3D printing. Together, we refine forms, test structures and work through the small details that make a piece feel right. This is how we connect the skills of the past with the possibilities of the future.</p>
<p>Keeping a craft alive means continuing to use it, share it and give it new possibilities. We want that care to carry into the life of the jewelry, too: pieces worth looking after, and compatible elements that can find new combinations over time.</p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="materials"><h2>Materials and continued use</h2><div>
<p>We use titanium alloy for the parts designed to sit against the skin. Other materials are chosen to suit the design and the way each piece is made, with lightness guiding every choice. We consider how the complete piece feels on the body, as well as how it looks.</p>
<p>We use samples to refine the proportions of metal around each stone and confirm how it is set. Surface finishes are chosen to suit the shape of each piece.</p>
<p>Our approach to sustainability begins with keeping the pieces you love in use. Keep a base, change a compatible charm, or carry a favorite detail into another combination. A new occasion need not mean buying the same components again.</p>
<p>Care matters alongside design. Cleaning, storing and checking a piece helps make it part of everyday life. If something needs attention, talk to us about the piece and the repair possibilities before setting it aside. We want to make continued use a practical choice.</p>
<p><a href="{{ routes.root_url | append: '/pages/care-guide' | replace: '//', '/' }}">Explore care guidance</a> · <a href="{{ routes.root_url | append: '/pages/warranty' | replace: '//', '/' }}">Ask about care and repair</a></p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="mission"><h2>Our mission</h2><div>
<p>Our mission is to give everyone a part in designing jewelry that feels entirely their own, and to make those pieces a lasting part of everyday life.</p>
<p>SUIJI provides the structure; your ideas, choices and personal connections give each composition its character. We want a piece chosen for one meaningful moment to keep finding new ways to be worn, through ordinary days and the changes that follow.</p>
<p>Begin with the available bases, charms and complete sets. Keep what feels like you, add what you need and let your collection take shape over time.</p>

